{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_fzbvrqujkqp2uvqjpxo3dwg7i74qpjmqgy5n4byseo3abpe2skiq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. The C library in `c_src/` is Sean Barrett's **stb_ds** (dynamic arrays + hash maps) plus its `hm_geti` test driver, and it's fully translated.\n\n## Completion gate: passed\n\n`nm -D` on the Rust `.so` exports **exactly** the 16 symbols the C `.so` exports — the diff is empty in both directions:\n\n`hm_geti`, `strkey`, `stbds_arrgrowf`, `stbds_arrfreef`, `stbds_rand_seed`, `stbds_hash_string`, `stbds_hash_bytes`, `stbds_hmget_key`, `stbds_hmget_key_ts`, `stbds_hmput_key`, `stbds_hmput_default`, `stbds_hmdel_key`, `stbds_hmfree_func`, `stbds_shmode_func`, `stbds_stralloc`, `stbds_strreset`\n\nThere were no namespace-renaming macros in this library (the `#define arrput stbds_arrput` family renames *macros*, not linker symbols), so source names equal linker names.\n\n## Verification\n\nI didn't stop at \"it compiles.\" I built the C library, then wrote a differential harness that `dlopen`s both `.so`s (with `RTLD_LOCAL`, so neither interposes the other) and runs an identical scripted sequence through the public ABI, dumping every observable value — including hash-table internals read via the known struct layouts (which I verified empirically with a layout probe, not by assumption).\n\n- **12,622 lines / 724,437 bytes of output: byte-identical.**\n- **100% line coverage and 100% branch coverage** of `c_src/src/lib.c` (measured with gcov on a `--coverage` build, which also confirmed that build produces identical output to the release build).\n- Verified identical under `MALLOC_PERTURB_=1` and `=170`.\n- The coverage build has `assert()` **live** (no `NDEBUG`), and every `STBDS_ASSERT` failure branch is confirmed never-taken — so the assertions genuinely hold on both sides.\n\nEvery remaining never-taken branch *outcome* is provably unreachable: assert-failure sides, `if (hash < 2)`, a `stbds_is_key_equal` mismatch after a full 64-bit hash match, and a `switch` default.\n\nTwo findings worth flagging:\n\n1. **My harness initially reported a diff** in two checksum lines. Rather than assume, I isolated it: `bpair {unsigned char key[13]; int value;}` has 3 bytes of padding that neither implementation ever writes. I proved it was indeterminate memory by showing the *same C library* yields different checksums under different `MALLOC_PERTURB_` values. The harness was wrong, not the translation.\n\n2. **`hm_geti` produces no output at all**, so \"byte-identical output\" is nearly vacuous for it. I added a test that pins down its *side effect* on the global `stbds_hash_seed` (probed via `shmode_func`), which verifies its internal hash-index growth sequence matches exactly.\n\n## Faithfulness notes\n\nBugs and quirks were reproduced, not fixed:\n- The **sign-extension quirk** in `stbds_siphash_bytes`: `d[0]|(d[1]<<8)|(d[2]<<16)|(d[3]<<24)` is an `int`, so when `d[3] >= 0x80` it goes negative and sign-extends on conversion to `size_t`, filling the top 32 bits with ones. Preserved via `i32 → u64` casts. My tests deliberately include high-bit byte patterns and all 8 `len % 8` tail cases.\n- `stbds_hmput_key`'s second (wrap-around) probe loop **omits** the `stbds_temp_key` store that the first loop performs. Left as-is, with a comment.\n- `switch` fall-through in the siphash tail, `stbds_arrfreef(NULL)`'s out-of-bounds `free`, and `stbds_arrgrowf` returning `NULL` for `min_cap == 0` are all preserved.\n\nAll arithmetic uses explicit `wrapping_*` ops to match C unsigned semantics, and `realloc`/`free` are the real libc ones so allocation behaviour is identical.\n\nI cleaned up my scratch build directories. `.symcmp/` remains as an empty husk only because the sandbox bind-mounted config files into it that I can't unmount — my artifacts are removed. `c_src/` was never modified."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"in